{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And the men came upon the women, etc.</b> One needs to know the intent of His saying <b>upon the women.</b> Further, why did He repeat saying a second time <b>all of a willing heart.</b> And perhaps it is because Scripture informs us of the manner of heartfelt generosity that the men of valor performed, for it is a well-known thing that there are gradations in the love of possessions, and they are four: the first of the firsts is the jewelry of a person's household members and the utensils of the house — these things a person values more than any acquisition of money, and the matter is simple. Second — gold, for of all acquisitions that exist in the world, one loves gold more than any of them. Third — things that are unavailable except in one's possession, their worth grows in one's eyes beyond their assessed value. Fourth — these are the rest of a person's acquisitions, silver and items equivalent to silver." "And Scripture arranged the gradations of the donors among the people, and the first in the realm of holiness it began with and said <b>and the men came upon the women</b> — meaning, whatever was upon the women, every generous-hearted person brought them. And Scripture arranged the ornaments they brought to show the devotion of the donors, for they did not wish to bring anything other than the most excellent and precious thing to them, even though they are subject to breakage and ruin; nevertheless, their souls' love for the holiness surpassed every precious thing their eyes beheld, and they brought every ornament of their women — חָח and נֶזֶם and טַבַּעַת, etc. And He was precise to say <b>nediv lev</b> (a generous heart) — know that there is a difference between יִדְּבֶנּוּ לִבּוֹ (his heart moved him) and נְדִיב לֵב, for יִדְּבֶנּוּ לִבּוֹ is said of anything one donates from one's heart, and sometimes one's heart moves him much and sometimes a little; but נְדִיב לֵב is said of a generous heart, and this is alluded to in His saying <b>whose heart lifted him</b>, for one whose heart is generous donates beyond his capacity and does not fret over the shortfall he may find, for he is happier in the giving than in the acquisition at home. And His saying <b>every golden vessel</b> — meaning, do not say that because they had the ornaments, that is why they brought them to HaShem the duplicate; therefore it says <b>every golden vessel</b> — they left themselves no golden vessel. And His saying <b>חָח and נֶזֶם</b> is the name of the type. Or it may intend <b>every golden vessel</b> to say that they left in their house no type of golden vessel with which a person takes pleasure, to fulfill His saying (Song of Songs 1:4) ״נָגִילָה וְנִשְׂמְחָה בָּךְ״. And perhaps it is that He alluded in His saying <b>upon the women</b> that they brought them upon them, and when she came before Moses they removed ornaments from upon them and gave them to the terumah of HaShem — an extraordinary devotion became known to them thereby toward our Master, blessed be He." "The second gradation is gold that is not fashioned as an ornament; concerning one who brings from this perspective He said <b>and every man who waved a wave-offering of gold to HaShem.</b> Third — things that are not to be found, for they were in the wilderness and there was nothing available there — תְּכֵלֶת and אַרְגָּמָן, etc.; and concerning one who brings from among them He said <b>and every man who</b>, etc., <b>תְּכֵלֶת וְאַרְגָּמָן</b>, etc. Fourth — and it is the gradation lower than all of them in the category of acquisitions, silver and items equivalent to silver; He said <b>whoever raises a terumah of silver and bronze</b>, etc." "And His saying <b>and all who were found with him acacia wood</b> — these have no designation of donation, and the reason is according to what they of blessed memory said (Tanhuma, Terumah) that Jacob our father foresaw, etc., and commanded and planted them for the needs of the Tabernacle; and therefore it said nothing but <b>and all who were found with him acacia wood</b>, etc. — <b>they brought.</b> And He was precise to say <b>for all the work of the service</b> to vindicate our words, for the presence of acacia wood in their hands was for the needs of the work of the Tabernacle, for if not, there would have been no need to say so; and furthermore, He should have preceded to say <b>they brought</b> and only then say <b>for all the work of the Tabernacle</b>; rather, it says that their presence in their hands was for the work, etc., and they did nothing but the bringing, and it is like a thing that involves no outlay of pocket for them — and understand. And according to what we have proposed there is a proper reason why He said in each and every detail ״they brought״ and did not suffice with one, for the gradation of the bringing is not equal."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:37:08.316828586Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}